WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:02.820
- If you want to learn to drive a train,
there are simulators.

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:05.510
A screen, a set of controls,
it's pretty realistic.

00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:09.050
But that's not the only safety-critical
job on the railway that needs training.

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:12.310
The signalers who decide where trains go
are just as important,

00:00:12.310 --> 00:00:14.530
making sure that every train
goes down the right track

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:16.260
and dealing with
disruption when it happens

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:18.770
so that passengers can still
get where they're going.

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:21.730
You could simulate that
in a computer, sure,

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:23.530
but there is a more physical approach.

00:00:23.530 --> 00:00:25.430
Here in Darmstadt, Germany,

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:28.580
there is the world's most
useful model railway.

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:34.530
- In this place, we started in 2006 at
the Technical University in Darmstadt.

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:38.010
We had a similar facility since 1936.

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:40.970
We have about 500 square metres
of space available

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:43.350
for the trainings, and a track laid out.

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:47.810
It's a fictional layout, 1.2 kilometres.
We have some fictional names.

00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:51.100
Some of the stations
are named after persons

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:53.940
that helped developing this facility.

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:57.320
We are training actual signalmen here.
They are taking exams here.

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:01.380
- So these are the oldest systems
on the Deutsche Bahn network?

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:04.440
- Okay, yeah, it's right.
It's mechanical interlocking.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:06.360
Everything is worked manually.

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:09.990
The blue levers are for the points,
the red levers are for signals.

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:11.833
- And that's our train?
- Yeah, that's our train.

00:01:11.833 --> 00:01:14.730
- And it's going that way.
- This way, right track.

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:16.350
- Not into that tiny train.

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:17.260
- That's right.
- Okay.

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:20.970
- So I have to know which
points have to be turned.

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:25.077
It's point number one and two.
So if you want to try it.

00:01:25.077 --> 00:01:28.220
- So, yep.
- You have to take it and turn it around.

00:01:30.477 --> 00:01:31.890
- All the way down.
- Just to lower position, yep.

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:33.640
- And, oh!

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:34.780
- It's turning over there.

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:35.613
- Okay.

00:01:35.613 --> 00:01:39.460
- So we have also point
number 7, 8, 9, and 10.

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:41.650
- Okay, so every time I do this...

00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:42.750
- One point will turn.

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:46.608
Just a simulation.
- Yes.

00:01:46.608 --> 00:01:49.900
But it's an accurate one.
- But it works like a real signal box.

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:51.800
- 'Cause in the real world,
I would be pulling that

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:53.780
and that will be pulling
through pulleys and levers

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:55.210
and actually physically
switching the point.

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:56.390
- Yeah.
- All right.

00:01:56.390 --> 00:01:59.640
- So here we just have a computer
who is turning the model points.

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:01.500
- Right, so you lock that into place,

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:04.410
it detects that I've done that,
and the computer goes "pht".

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:05.580
- Yeah, that's it.
- Okay.

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:09.043
So we've set the points, the
next is safety interlock.

00:02:09.043 --> 00:02:10.263
And that's a German thing?

00:02:10.263 --> 00:02:13.920
- This is a German thing.
Well, it's just common in German.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.560
We have what we call a route locking.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:20.620
So we want to open a special signal,
so we have to take the right route lever,

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:22.120
which is called P2.

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:23.837
- Because it says 1, 2, 7, 8, 9, 10,

00:02:23.837 --> 00:02:24.670
which is what I've just moved.
- Yep, that's it.

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:26.763
- So do I pull up, down?
- Down, down please.

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:31.056
- So this lever checks if all
the points are set correct,

00:02:31.056 --> 00:02:34.100
and second thing, he
will lock all the points.

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:35.630
So we can't move them back.

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:37.360
- What happens if I were
to pull the wrong lever?

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:38.400
- Just try it.
- Right, okay.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:39.830
- Try it, it won't work.

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:40.663
- Oh!
- It's closed.

00:02:40.663 --> 00:02:42.780
- It's a physical lock.
- Yep.

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:44.480
- I kind of assumed that
was just going to beep at me

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:45.899
or complain, but that's an actual

00:02:45.899 --> 00:02:47.570
physical interlock.
- This is real, physical.

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:50.423
- Right, so now I can't even-
- Yeah, try it.

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:56.010
It's not possible to pull them back.
- You can't even close the lever.

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:58.000
- Yep.
- Wow, okay. So that's-

00:02:58.000 --> 00:02:59.500
- So this is an old computer.

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:02.600
It checks if everything is
set at the right position.

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:03.740
- Wow, okay.

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:07.150
So we have put the points in position,

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:08.790
interlocked everything
to make sure it's safe.

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:09.770
- That's right.

00:03:09.770 --> 00:03:10.840
- And then we just-

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:13.413
- Last step is put the
signal to an off position.

00:03:13.413 --> 00:03:15.990
It's P2.
- Which is going to be...

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:18.790
- The P2.
- P2, all right.

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:22.414
- Just like point levers
to an off position.

00:03:22.414 --> 00:03:23.640
[machine beeping]

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:25.547
And now the train driver
sees a green signal,

00:03:25.547 --> 00:03:27.040
and he can move on.

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:30.200
- But it takes a few seconds
and the computer kind of simulates...

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:33.270
- It's a simulation, because
he has to release his brakes

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:35.030
before he can start.

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:36.240
- So what's the last thing we need to do?

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:39.250
- So now we have to lock the line
so another train can't follow.

00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:42.503
So we have to tell us
and the next signal box

00:03:42.503 --> 00:03:44.120
there's a train on the line.

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:48.520
So we have to put down the button
and to produce some electricity.

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:50.245
- Oh!
- Just put it down.

00:03:50.245 --> 00:03:52.829
- Pull it down.
- And 10 times, please.

00:03:52.829 --> 00:03:55.412
[box whirring]

00:03:56.530 --> 00:03:59.630
- So that would have sent
a bell ringing or something

00:03:59.630 --> 00:04:01.540
in another signal box saying
we've got a train coming.

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:02.373
- Yep, that's it.

00:04:02.373 --> 00:04:05.700
- So that's the manual
ones. What came next?

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:07.740
- Interlocking, which is
called electromechanical.

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:10.376
That's the next thing, which
was about 50 years later.

00:04:10.376 --> 00:04:12.070
- All right, let's do that.

00:04:12.070 --> 00:04:14.380
- We've got the next station,
which is an all-electric

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:17.250
interlocking from the 1950s.

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:19.600
Our train is coming, we see the red arrow.

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:22.880
It's a train intersection marker.

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:25.310
Train will stop at the
entry signal of the station.

00:04:25.310 --> 00:04:27.510
- Because that's a red and green light,
and there's a red light there.

00:04:27.510 --> 00:04:30.330
And this is the station. These
are all the tracks, okay.

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:31.990
- So we can see all the tracks.

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:35.640
You can see in the
direction of the points.

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:39.590
And now we want this train to enter
into track number two.

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:44.170
So, now if you want to operate a point,
we have to do a two button operation,

00:04:44.170 --> 00:04:47.920
which means you have to
push the button of the...

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:48.753
- Yep.

00:04:48.753 --> 00:04:50.573
- And second one is this blue one.

00:04:50.573 --> 00:04:53.620
Now it starts to blink.
The point is turning.

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:55.660
And now it's on the left.

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:56.493
- And now it's on the left.

00:04:56.493 --> 00:04:58.700
So the train is now going the right way.

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:00.330
So if there's nothing
else in this section,

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:01.560
you can then turn that to green?

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:05.690
- Yep. So, we have to make an
entry-exit operation.

00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:07.720
We are starting at the entry signal

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:09.500
and we want to move into track number two.

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:11.090
There are two buttons.

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:12.810
- Right.
- Try it.

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:14.580
- So I have to push that one?

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:17.010
- Yep, and same time track number two.

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:19.784
- These two.
- Yep, and nothing happens.

00:05:19.784 --> 00:05:21.410
- Okay!

00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:25.470
- Because we are missing
two another points.

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:28.098
We have a flank protection,
which is missing.

00:05:28.098 --> 00:05:30.500
There might be a train waiting here

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:33.500
and he might just start
to roll into our train.

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:34.530
- And he'll, right.

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:37.430
So I need to change that one as well.

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:40.150
So now there's no way that a
train coming the other way-

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:41.770
- That's right.
- Can come in, okay.

00:05:41.770 --> 00:05:46.140
- And second point, we have to
operate as point number one,

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:48.210
which is called the overlap.

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:52.100
So if a train won't stop
at an exit signal...

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:54.550
- Yep, it'll derail there
at the moment, won't it?

00:05:54.550 --> 00:05:57.490
- Yes, and maybe he
could hit another train,

00:05:57.490 --> 00:06:01.010
so we have to reserve this
space also for the train.

00:06:01.010 --> 00:06:03.130
- Okay. So that one?
- Yep.

00:06:03.130 --> 00:06:04.020
Which is called the overlap.

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:06.730
- All right.
- So, now try it again.

00:06:06.730 --> 00:06:10.010
Entry exit operation.
- Entry exit.

00:06:10.010 --> 00:06:12.220
Lights up? Green light.
- Lights up green light.

00:06:12.220 --> 00:06:15.710
Train will enter in the next,
I don't know, one, two minutes.

00:06:15.710 --> 00:06:17.380
- And somewhere out
there on the big model,

00:06:17.380 --> 00:06:19.123
a train has started moving.

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:21.650
That's lovely.

00:06:21.650 --> 00:06:24.720
And this is all electrical circuits.

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:27.980
This isn't microcontrollers,
this is all physical wiring.

00:06:27.980 --> 00:06:29.420
- Or relay interlocking.

00:06:29.420 --> 00:06:32.690
- There is really something
in having that view

00:06:32.690 --> 00:06:35.480
of what you're doing versus a screen

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:37.740
and a vague idea in a diagram.

00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:41.090
Like, I can see the train
pulling into the station.

00:06:41.090 --> 00:06:43.350
That's lovely.
So that's the 1950s,

00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:45.540
and there's still some of
this equipment out there.

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:46.620
- Of course.
- Of course.

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:48.230
What's the most modern version?

00:06:48.230 --> 00:06:51.400
- That's the computer interlocking.
And now I will show you.

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:52.233
- Lead the way.

00:06:53.290 --> 00:06:55.040
- All of the interlockings
that you used today

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:58.040
are still in use in the
German rail network.

00:06:58.040 --> 00:07:01.160
The numbers, for example, of
the mechanical interlockings

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:03.350
are dwindling at the moment.

00:07:03.350 --> 00:07:05.610
In the future, all of
them will be replaced

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:07.810
by electronic or computer interlockings.

00:07:07.810 --> 00:07:09.870
The physical railway that you crash

00:07:09.870 --> 00:07:12.350
will always stay in your mind,

00:07:12.350 --> 00:07:14.490
even if it's a model train!

00:07:14.490 --> 00:07:17.300
And you have the ability
to get an overview

00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:20.770
about the situation during
any sort of disruptions,

00:07:20.770 --> 00:07:23.549
not only the really focused
view that you would have

00:07:23.549 --> 00:07:25.500
during this disruption.

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:27.750
- So this is the future?
- Yep, that's the future.

00:07:27.750 --> 00:07:29.607
- So which train are we moving?

00:07:29.607 --> 00:07:32.350
- We are try moving a
train which is called 201.

00:07:32.350 --> 00:07:33.730
- 201, okay. Yeah, I see it.

00:07:33.730 --> 00:07:38.320
- So all you have to do is an
entry exit operation, with a mouse.

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:41.670
- Oh, so just the single
button push from earlier?

00:07:41.670 --> 00:07:42.656
Everything else is automatic?

00:07:42.656 --> 00:07:44.545
- Everything else is going automatically.

00:07:44.545 --> 00:07:47.470
So first thing is you have
to move the mouse pointer

00:07:47.470 --> 00:07:51.020
to the red triangle on
the right side, please.

00:07:51.020 --> 00:07:53.020
It's the exit signal for the station.

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:55.693
And now move on to just one mouse click.

00:07:56.900 --> 00:07:59.805
And now next train, please, on the right.

00:07:59.805 --> 00:08:01.810
- The blue-
- The blue one to the right.

00:08:01.810 --> 00:08:03.150
That's the exit.

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:05.010
- And click that?
- Click there.

00:08:05.010 --> 00:08:06.080
- I don't know why I'm being so nervous.

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:09.210
It's a model train set. But
in my head, this is serious.

00:08:09.210 --> 00:08:12.390
So it's just done all the button
pushes that I did earlier.

00:08:12.390 --> 00:08:13.750
All the lever pulls.
- Yep.

00:08:13.750 --> 00:08:14.583
- Two mouse clicks.

00:08:14.583 --> 00:08:17.140
- At the moment, nothing has operated.

00:08:17.140 --> 00:08:22.790
It's just, "I want to go," from
track number five onto the line.

00:08:22.790 --> 00:08:24.150
- And it's doing all the interlocking?

00:08:24.150 --> 00:08:25.475
- Yeah.
- Making sure that

00:08:25.475 --> 00:08:26.308
nothing's in the way.
- And now have to tell

00:08:26.308 --> 00:08:27.795
the computer, "Okay, I want to do it."

00:08:27.795 --> 00:08:31.600
There's a button which is called in German
"Verarbeiten" which means OK.

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:34.800
And after you push it,
all the points are moving.

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:36.245
- All at the same time.
- Yep.

00:08:36.245 --> 00:08:37.697
And the signal is going to...

00:08:37.697 --> 00:08:38.910
- And the signal's gone to green.

00:08:38.910 --> 00:08:39.790
- Yep. That's it.

00:08:39.790 --> 00:08:44.140
- It absolutely makes sense,
but also it seems so much easier.

00:08:44.140 --> 00:08:47.640
I know it's not. You've got exactly
the same checks here.

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:48.840
- Of course.
- That were being done

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:52.790
by the systems, that were
being done by the circuits

00:08:52.790 --> 00:08:55.130
and then being done by
the physical interlocks.

00:08:55.130 --> 00:08:57.810
But here it's code and we
can see the train progress.

00:08:57.810 --> 00:09:01.370
Like, the lights go red when
there's a train in the section.

00:09:01.370 --> 00:09:02.340
That's so clever.

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:06.608
- Big difference is I do have to operate
2, 3, 4, 5 stations.

00:09:06.608 --> 00:09:08.260
[Tom laughs]

00:09:08.260 --> 00:09:11.010
So it's not as simple as it looks like.

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:11.930
- Right.

00:09:11.930 --> 00:09:13.200
- You know the computer game?

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:17.220
- You have to do the same amount of work,
just over a much bigger area.

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:18.520
- That's it.
- All right.

00:09:19.690 --> 00:09:21.290
Thank you so much.
- Thank you.

